          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-131303",
          ":question/problem": "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & \u2194{R}_{4} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."
